966-4.05c.58,0,1.098-.145,1.553-.435.455-.29.85-.672,1.186-1.147.335-.475.612-1,.832-1.575s.383-1.152.487-1.732c.105-.58.158-1.115.158-1.605,0-.68-.104-1.232-.308-1.658-.205-.425-.482-.74-.833-.945-.35-.205-.755-.308-1.215-.308-.58,0-1.1.145-1.56.435s-.858.672-1.192,1.147c-.336.475-.613,1-.833,1.575-.22.575-.385,1.153-.495,1.733s-.165,1.115-.165,1.605c0,.68.105,1.235.315,1.665.21.43.497.745.862.945s.768.3,1.207.3Z\" stroke-width=\"0\"></path></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for triangle upper P upper Q upper R\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>The measure of angle upper P is 24°.</li><li>The measure of angle upper Q is 132°.</li><li>The measure of angle upper R is x°.</li><li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li></ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the triangle shown, <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q equals upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["57adc815-4355-4b5e-8fa1-1e6602f1482a"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"164353ec-5ca9-46a8-bacc-4d01343fd681","content":"<p><math alttext=\"156\"><mn>156</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"89e0532f-4d07-419a-b53b-9cf1232ebc95","content":"<p><math alttext=\"66\"><mn>66</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e7b601d7-7115-4814-ba72-7f260e0c23b2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"48\"><mn>48</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"57adc815-4355-4b5e-8fa1-1e6602f1482a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is correct. The sum of the angle measures of a triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"132 degree\"><mn>132</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>,&nbsp;the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"24 degree\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x degree\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x plus 132 plus 24 equals 180\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>132</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x plus 152 equals 180\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>152</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"152\"><mn>152</mn></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 24\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Alternate approach: It&rsquo;s given that in the triangle shown, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P upper Q equals upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>. Thus, triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is an isosceles triangle. Since base angles of an isosceles triangle are congruent, the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math> is equal to the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>. Therefore, the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"24 degree\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum of the measures, in degrees, of angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math>, not the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the sum of the measures, in degrees, of angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>, not the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>.</p>","externalid":"96a4ec26-6ad9-4b3b-9ae1-693feee5691e","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1776362727993,"updateDate":1776362727993}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727993, 1776362727993),
    ($SATQ$739f1bbc$SATQ$, $SATQ$b693eec7-7a41-4127-adf6-bb6bf263019b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">In triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, <math alttext="upper A upper B equals 4,680 millimeters left parenthesis mm right parenthesis"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>millimeters</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mtext>mm</mtext></mfenced></math> and <math alttext="upper B upper C equals 4,680 mm"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math>. Which statement is sufficient to prove that triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is equilateral?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"06d78baa-1b42-45cd-9719-4630d2d565a3","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 4,680 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"},{"id":"1029804f-90b6-4c63-98b2-b8b6eca3a574","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 468 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>468</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"},{"id":"4650e282-0bba-4e9e-bf2c-ba4036df7ce0","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 46.8 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>46.8</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"},{"id":"3b9443dd-93af-4dab-90fe-3fe4f087a883","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 4.68 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4.68</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["06d78baa-1b42-45cd-9719-4630d2d565a3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. In an equilateral triangle, all three sides have the same length. It&rsquo;s given that in triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, <math alttext="upper A upper B equals 4,680 millimeters"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,680</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math> and <math alttext="upper B upper C equals 4,680 millimeters"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,680</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math>. Therefore, if <math alttext="upper A upper C equals 4,680 millimeters"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,680</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math>, then all three sides of triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> have the same length, so triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is equilateral. Therefore, <math alttext="upper A upper C equals 4,680 millimeters"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,680</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math> is sufficient to prove that triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is equilateral.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"739f1bbc","external_id":"b693eec7-7a41-4127-adf6-bb6bf263019b","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"d785c8fd-df1a-41c8-a9f2-f0097230e717","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"upper A upper B equals 4,680 millimeters left parenthesis mm right parenthesis\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>millimeters</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mtext>mm</mtext></mfenced></math> and <math alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 4,680 mm\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math>. Which statement is sufficient to prove that triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is equilateral?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"06d78baa-1b42-45cd-9719-4630d2d565a3","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 4,680 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4,680</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"},{"id":"1029804f-90b6-4c63-98b2-b8b6eca3a574","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 468 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>468</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></math></p>"},{"id":"4650e282-0bba-4e9e-bf2c-ba4036df7ce0","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 46.8 mm\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4