</math>. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. In triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math>, it's given that angle <math alttext="upper Y"><mi>Y</mi>
</math> is a right angle. Thus, <math alttext="line segment upper X upper Y"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg opposite of angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="line segment upper Y upper Z"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math>. It follows that <math alttext="tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's also given that the measure of angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="33 degree"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> and the length of <math alttext="line segment upper Y upper Z"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext="26"><mn>26</mn>
</math> units. Substituting <math alttext="33 degree"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> for <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="26"><mn>26</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="upper Y upper Z"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext="tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext="tangent 33 degree  equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over 26 EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mn>26</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext="26"><mn>26</mn>
