</math> and the length of <math alttext="line segment upper X upper Y"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext="26 tangent 33 degree"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, it follows that the area of triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext="one half left parenthesis 26 right parenthesis left parenthesis 26 tangent 33 degree right parenthesis"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mn>26</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></mrow></mfenced></math> square units, or <math alttext="338 tangent 33 degree"><mn>338</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> square units. It's given that the area, in square units, of triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> can be represented by the expression <math alttext="k tangent 33 degree"><mi>k</mi><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, where <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> is a constant. Therefore, <math alttext="338"><mn>338</mn>
</math> is the value of <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
