angle. Thus, <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg opposite of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math>. It follows that <math alttext=\"tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's also given that the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"33 degree\"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> units. Substituting <math alttext=\"33 degree\"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> for <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext=\"tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"tangent 33 degree  equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over 26 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mn>26</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree  equals upper X upper Y\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math>. Therefore, the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. The area of a triangle is half the product of the lengths of its legs. Since the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, it follows that the area of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 26 right parenthesis left parenthesis 26 tangent 33 degree right parenthesis\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mn>26</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></mrow></mfenced></math> square units, or <math alttext=\"338 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>338</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> square units. It's given that the area, in square units, of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> can be represented by the expression <math alttext=\"k tangent 33 degree\"><mi>k</mi><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, where <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is a constant. Therefore, <math alttext=\"338\"><mn>338</mn>\n</math> is the value of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1743430554988,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"85f1892d","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1743430554988,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"e8f7d348-9106-4f42-a41c-8400c1758a07","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"2dac0d11-8752-481a-b680-d21a726542f2","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angle <math alttext=\"upper Y\"><mi>Y</mi>\n</math> is a right angle, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"33 degree\"><mrow><mn>33</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> units. If the area, in square units, of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> can be represented by the expression <math alttext=\"k tangent 33 degree\"><mi>k</mi><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mn>33</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, where <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is a constant, what is the value of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["338"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"338\"><mn>338</mn>\n</math>. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. In triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math>, it's given that angle <math alttext=\"upper Y\"><mi>Y</mi>\n</math> is a right angle. Thus, <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg opposite of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math>. It follows that <math alttext=\"tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's also given that the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"33 degree\"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> units. Substituting <math alttext=\"33 degree\"><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> for <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext=\"tangent upper Z equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over upper Y upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"tangent 33 degree  equals StartFraction upper X upper Y Over 26 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mn>26</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree  equals upper X upper Y\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math>. Therefore, the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. The area of a triangle is half the product of the lengths of its legs. Since the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math> and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"26 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, it follows that the area of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 26 right parenthesis left parenthesis 26 tangent 33 degree right parenthesis\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mn>26</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>26</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></mrow></mfenced></math> square units, or <math alttext=\"338 tangent 33 degree\"><mn>338</mn><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> square units. It's given that the area, in square units, of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> can be represented by the expression <math alttext=\"k tangent 33 degree\"><mi>k</mi><mi>tan</mi><mn>33</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, where <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is a constant. Therefore, <math alttext=\"338\"><mn>338</mn>\n</math> is the value of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>.</p>","externalid":"e8f7d348-9106-4f42-a41c-8400c1758a07","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["338"]},"createDate":1743430554988,"updateDate":1743430554988}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554988, 1743430554988),
